WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.005
This lesson walks the production RAG pipeline, stage by stage.

2
00:00:04.335 --> 00:00:07.805
Most hallucination complaints are really retrieval failures.

3
00:00:08.605 --> 00:00:13.312
Picture a solar company whose assistant helps technicians read installation manuals.

4
00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:16.685
Up on a roof, a technician asks for a clamp's torque setting.

5
00:00:17.485 --> 00:00:20.123
The assistant confidently gives the wrong number.

6
00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:24.222
A bigger model won't help: the spec was lost before the model saw it.

7
00:00:25.022 --> 00:00:28.056
The pipeline has seven stages: extract, chunk,

8
00:00:28.106 --> 00:00:31.073
embed, index, retrieve, rerank, and generate.

9
00:00:31.403 --> 00:00:34.084
Each one fails in its own recognizable way.

10
00:00:34.884 --> 00:00:37.293
Extraction is where most quality is lost:

11
00:00:37.343 --> 00:00:40.231
the torque table became a run-on line of numbers.

12
00:00:40.561 --> 00:00:43.749
Table-aware parsing, like NeMo Retriever extraction,

13
00:00:43.799 --> 00:00:45.866
keeps each value under its header.

14
00:00:46.666 --> 00:00:50.904
Chunking should follow headings, sections, and tables, with a small overlap.

15
00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:56.091
Every chunk carries metadata: source, section, date, access level, and version.

16
00:00:56.891 --> 00:01:01.022
Chunks too small lose their context; too large, and the embedding blurs.

17
00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:05.185
Small-to-big searches small chunks, but returns the whole parent section.

18
00:01:05.985 --> 00:01:09.988
Question-answer embedders encode questions and passages differently.

19
00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:14.236
Encode a query as if it were a passage, and recall silently drops.

20
00:01:15.036 --> 00:01:18.335
Changing the embedding model means re-embedding everything.

21
00:01:18.665 --> 00:01:21.794
Vectors from two different models simply can't be compared.

22
00:01:22.594 --> 00:01:27.237
Vector search misses exact tokens, like the inverter error code E-42.

23
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:32.381
Hybrid search adds keyword search, and fuses both lists with Reciprocal Rank Fusion.

24
00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:35.921
Metadata filters run before the vector search,

25
00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:38.529
so restricted manuals are never candidates.

26
00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:42.201
Filtering afterwards leaks what exists and wastes recall.

27
00:01:43.001 --> 00:01:47.089
A cross-encoder reranker is accurate, but too slow for a whole corpus.

28
00:01:47.419 --> 00:01:51.828
So retrieve the top fifty cheaply, then rerank down to the best three to five.

29
00:01:52.628 --> 00:01:57.101
Finally, the prompt says: answer only from the sources, and cite every claim.

30
00:01:57.431 --> 00:01:59.813
If they don't cover it, say so instead of guessing.

31
00:02:00.613 --> 00:02:04.209
The trap: a reranker only reorders what retrieval returned,

32
00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:06.557
so it can't rescue a missing document.

33
00:02:06.887 --> 00:02:11.467
Fix recall upstream, with hybrid search, better chunking, or query rewriting.

34
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:15.217
On the exam, wrong table numbers mean extraction,

35
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:17.422
and missed codes mean hybrid search.

36
00:02:17.752 --> 00:02:19.558
Read on for each stage in detail.
